WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:08.433
Ryan: Hi, I'd like to tell you
about Google Cloud Identity

00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:09.666
and Access Management.

00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:11.400
I'll call it Cloud IAM,
or IAM for short.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.066
Cloud IAM is Google Cloud
Platform's unified system

00:00:14.066 --> 00:00:17.433
for managing access to resources
and assigning permissions

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:21.300
for users and services to access
those resources.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.000
Cloud IAM is designed
for organizations

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.133
with many individual projects
and users.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:28.666
The idea is to unify control
for access

00:00:28.666 --> 00:00:32.066
for all of these projects
and the resources in one place,

00:00:32.066 --> 00:00:33.933
making it easier
for one organization

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.966
to keep track of everything
they've got going on

00:00:35.966 --> 00:00:37.333
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:39.733
IAM is built on a concept
called a policy.

00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:41.266
Policies answer the question,

00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:43.766
"Who can do what
to which thing?"

00:00:43.766 --> 00:00:45.300
They are made up of permissions

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.533
which grant access to resources
that are bundled into roles

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.566
which are assigned
to identities.

00:00:49.566 --> 00:00:52.533
The combination of identity,
role, permission, and resource

00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:55.266
determine if a given action
is allowed by IAM.

00:00:55.266 --> 00:00:56.666
In order to further understand
IAM,

00:00:56.666 --> 00:00:59.666
we need to go into more depth
on identities, resources,

00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:01.733
permissions, and roles.

00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:05.066
Identity is all about defining
the "who" part of an IAM policy.

00:01:05.066 --> 00:01:07.033
Individual people
have Google accounts

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:09.266
that can be used as an identity
in IAM.

00:01:09.266 --> 00:01:11.066
Service accounts, which are used
for one service

00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:13.533
to connect another without
human intervention,

00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:15.466
are another type of identity.

00:01:15.466 --> 00:01:17.733
Both these types of accounts
have a username

00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:19.400
and some sort of credential.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.566
Both of these accounts can be
placed into Google Groups

00:01:21.566 --> 00:01:23.800
which can be used to simplify
assigning permissions

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.566
to resources.

00:01:25.566 --> 00:01:26.933
You can also use
a Google App Domain

00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:28.766
as you would a group to give
permission to a resource

00:01:28.766 --> 00:01:30.866
to all users
in a given organization.

00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:32.400
Resources are the various
components

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.433
of Google Cloud Platform to
which you can control access.

00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:37.933
Resources include things like
Cloud Platform projects,

00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:40.933
Cloud Storage Buckets,
and Pub/Sub topics.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.100
Check the documentation
for which resources

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.900
are available to use with IAM
at the current time.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.433
In IAM, permissions take the
form of service, resource, verb.

00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:51.833
So if I wanted to grant
an identity

00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:53.566
the right to listen to a topic
in Pub/Sub,

00:01:53.566 --> 00:01:56.900
the correct permission would be
pubsub.topic.list.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.233
If instead I wanted to grant
the ability to write a file

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:00.866
to a bucket in Cloud Storage,

00:02:00.866 --> 00:02:03.366
I would apply
storage.bucket.create.

00:02:03.366 --> 00:02:05.500
Check the documentation
for the service in question

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.933
as to what the various
permissions are.

00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:10.466
Roles are to permissions
as groups are for identities.

00:02:10.466 --> 00:02:11.533
They allow you to bundle up
a set

00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:12.866
of logically connected
permissions

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:15.466
in a common way and then add
them to a policy.

00:02:15.466 --> 00:02:17.266
If you are a Google Cloud
Platform veteran,

00:02:17.266 --> 00:02:18.533
then you are familiar with what

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.266
we are now calling
primitive roles:

00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:22.666
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:02:22.666 --> 00:02:25.166
These roles work exactly
the same way they always have,

00:02:25.166 --> 00:02:27.466
but in many cases they can
result in being more permissive

00:02:27.466 --> 00:02:29.000
than you want to be.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.533
For example, under this model
an editor in a given project

00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:33.233
can add VMs in Compute Engine

00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:35.866
and delete storage buckets
in Cloud Storage.

00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:38.700
This may be suboptimal
for your purposes.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.500
That's why we've added
curated roles.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.600
These curated roles are tied
to resources

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.200
and allow for a resource centric
assigning of access rates.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.000
For example, someone with
the role appengine.admin

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.966
has the equivalent of a broad
array or individual permissions

00:02:51.966 --> 00:02:53.766
that allow them to administer
a project's

00:02:53.766 --> 00:02:55.800
entire app engine
configuration.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.733
However, someone with
appengine.deployer

00:02:57.733 --> 00:03:00.433
can only add code and push
that code to production.

00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:02.800
They could not modify the daily
quota for app engine.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.733
Furthermore, neither of these
users could modify anything

00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:06.233
to do with Compute Engine.

00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:08.400
Not unless someone had given
them access to Compute Engine

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.633
separately with Compute Engine
roles.

00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:12.633
As you can see, policies
are made up of roles

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:16.066
which are made up permissions
which are assigned to resources.

00:03:16.066 --> 00:03:18.366
We add groups or users
to that policy,

00:03:18.366 --> 00:03:20.233
and through that policy
they gain access

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.800
to the specific resources
their roles give them.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.966
The last concept you should know
is hierarchy.

00:03:25.966 --> 00:03:28.600
Basically policies flow from
the top in organization

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.166
down to projects
and finally to resources,

00:03:31.166 --> 00:03:33.666
which means that you can assign
organization-wide policies

00:03:33.666 --> 00:03:35.433
which can then be augmented
by policies

00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:37.200
that you set on
a particular project,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.833
which can further be added to by
policies you set on a resource.

00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:42.966
So you can assign access
to individual resources

00:03:42.966 --> 00:03:45.166
or you can go higher and apply
them to an entire project

00:03:45.166 --> 00:03:47.666
or even higher and assign them
to an entire organization,

00:03:47.666 --> 00:03:49.966
which will then apply to all
projects in that organization

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:53.100
and those policies will cascade
down to resources

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.566
in those projects,

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:58.466
meaning you can be as permissive
or as locked down as you like.

00:03:58.466 --> 00:04:00.166
That's the basics of Cloud IAM.

00:04:00.166 --> 00:04:02.600
You can find out more
at our documentation website,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.166
and we can't wait to find out
what you're gonna build next

00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:06.466
with Cloud IAM.

